@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>Ignite is a Rust crate that adds an admin panel to a Rocket app. The app needs a Rust toolchain, a MariaDB or MySQL database, and, for the panel's assets, Node.js.</p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rust, stable, with Cargo.</li>
    <li>MariaDB or MySQL. Ignite supports no other database.</li>
    <li>Node.js 22.12 or newer, or 20.19 or newer, for Vite and Tailwind.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Pinned versions</h2>
  <p>Ignite, its docs and its example use the same versions of these crates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Rocket, from git, at revision <code>3a54d079</code>.</li>
    <li>Propel, <code>propel_rocket</code>, from git, at revision <code>1853048</code>. Propel renders the panel's views.</li>
    <li>Diesel 2.x, with the <code>mysql</code>, <code>r2d2</code>, <code>chrono</code> and <code>diesel_migrations</code> features.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use the same Rocket revision as Propel, so the app has one copy of Rocket:</p>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-toml">[dependencies]
rocket = { git = "https://github.com/rwf2/Rocket", rev = "3a54d079" }
propel_rocket = { git = "https://github.com/lajennylove/propel", rev = "18530480ee3328845b0abeaf35d0f8397f50634d" }
diesel = { version = "2", features = ["mysql", "r2d2", "chrono", "diesel_migrations"] }</code></pre>
  <p>The Ignite crates are not built yet, so this page does not give their dependency line.</p>

  <h2>Database URL</h2>
  <p><strong>Not built yet.</strong> Ignite will read the connection URL from the <code>DATABASE_URL</code> environment variable, in the form <code>mysql://user:pass@host:port/db</code>. It will open the connection pool when the app launches.</p>

  <h2>Migrations</h2>
  <p><strong>Not built yet.</strong> Ignite will ship a migration that creates the <code>users</code> table, the table the login uses. The app will run it with <code>diesel migration run</code>, or from its own code.</p>

  <h2>Attach the panel</h2>
  <p><strong>Not built yet.</strong> The panel will attach to the Rocket build in one call, which also mounts its routes, by default under <code>/admin</code>.</p>

  <h2>Create the first user</h2>
  <p><strong>Not built yet.</strong> A command-line tool will create the first user. It will ask for the password without showing it on screen.</p>

  <h2>Run it</h2>
  <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-bash">npm install
npm run build   # once: writes public/build, which Rocket serves
npm run dev     # Vite, in one terminal
cargo run       # the Rocket app, in another</code></pre>
  <p>Rocket will not start until <code>public/build</code> exists, so run <code>npm run build</code> once first. Views are read from <code>resources/views/</code> at runtime, so an edit to a view shows on the next reload.</p>
@endsection
